Troubleshooting {#chapter:troubleshooting}
===============

Autotools (`configure`)
-----------------------

-   **`configure` fails with ...**. Before you start any further
    investigation, update to the latest version of the autotools, ensure
    your configure script suits this version, and rerun the build system
    configuration:

    autoreconf --install libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp
    src/config.h.in .; automake --add-missing

-   **checking host system type\... Invalid configuration './configure':
    machine './configure-unknown' not recognized**. We got this recently
    on a number of new Linux installations. In this case, adding

    to your `configure` call seems to be necessary.

-   To be continued ...

Python API
----------

-   **`import peano4` yields not found error**. You haven't set your
    `PYTHONPATH` properly. Ensure that it points to 's `python`
    subdirectory. So use something similar to

    export PYTHONPATH= /git/Peano/python

-   **/usr/bin/ld: cannot find -lExaHyPE2Core3d\_trace**. Your Python
    script has not been able to locate the  libraries. You can either
    tweak your systems parameters, i.e. set the search pathes, or you
    can manually add the library:

-   My installation or paraview complains about **ModuleNotFoundError:
    No module named 'numpy'**. For most parts of , you need the package
    `numpy`, though major parts of  can operate without it. If you don't
    have numpy on your system install it with either of the following
    commands:

    pip4 install --user numpy python3 -m pip install --user --update pip

    Similar arguments hold for `jinja2`.

-   To be continued ...

Compilation
-----------

-   **My compiler yields warnings of the type**

    **How can I disable them?** Please consult your compiler options.
    Usually re-configuring with the flag
    `CXXFLAGS="... -Wno-unknown-attributes"` or
    `CXXFLAGS="... -Wno-attributes=clang::"` eliminates the warnings.

-   **My compiler reports something like**

    We have seen this error with GCC if the supported C++ generation is
    too old. Try to return configure with `CXXFLAGS=-std=c++0x`.
    Depending on your compiler, the flag might also be
    `CXXFLAGS=–std=c++0x` (two hyphens).

-   **My compiler yields errors like**

    'warning \#3191: scoped enumeration types are a C++11 feature'

    Your compiler seems not to have C++11 (or, newer) enabled by
    default. Rerun configure with the flag `CXXFLAGS=-std=c++0x`.

-   **My compiler terminates with**

    'error: unknown attribute 'optimize' ignored\*'

    We have seen this issue notably on macOS with CLANG replacing GCC.
    Unfortunately, CLANG seems to pretend to be GNU on some systems and
    then the wrong header is included. Ensure that
    `CompilerSpecificSettings.h`[^1] includes a file such that the
    compiler `CompilerCLANG` is defined.

-   **My compiler requires ages to translate the unit tests**. Ensure
    that the flag `UseTestSpecificCompilerSettings` is enabled. This
    effectively disables any optimisation for the unit tests. Without
    the flag, unit test can become really big due to template
    instantiation.

-   **`./configure` crashes when I try to translate with TBB**. I have
    seen this on a couple of systems and found it annoying. First,
    ensure that configure find the TBB header. To ensure it does, check
    that `CXXFLAGS` are properly set. Next, set `LDFLAGS=-Lmypath`.
    Usually, I add `-ltbb` to this parameter and then everything is
    fine. However, some configures then don't like the configure script
    anymore. So I instead use `export PEANO_LDFLAGS="-ltbb"`. For some
    systems, it then still remains necessary to add pthreads, too.

-   **My compiler terminates with errors when I enable OpenMP
    multithreading** . Make sure that you are using a compiler with
    OpenMP 5.0 support, such as GCC 9.x.

-   **I configured Peano with HDF5, but my application (experiment) will
    not compile**. The application will parse the configure call used by
    Peano to feed its Makefile, but will not infer the settings from
    `–with-hdf5`. To get the correct flags for HDF5 support for your
    experiment, you might want to add the following to your Peano
    configuration call:
    `LDFLAGS="-lhdf5_hl_cpp -lhdf5_cpp -lhdf5_hl -lhdf5 -lstdc++"`.
    Notice that some outdated GCC compilers might struggle with the last
    one on the list.

-   To be continued ...

Linking
-------

-   **My linker yields errors like 'undefined reference to std::vector
    \... '**. We have seen this with some outdated GCC compilers. Ensure
    you meet the minimum GCC requirements or switch to another compiler.

-   **The linker says that the vtk libraries were not found**. VTK can
    be tricky to get up and running. See a comprehensive discussion on
    VTK issues in Chapter
    [\[chapter:postprocessing\]](#chapter:postprocessing){reference-type="ref"
    reference="chapter:postprocessing"}. It includes compiler/linker
    issues.

-   **If I use Fortran as well, I get errors alike *undefined reference
    to for\_stop\_core' or 'for\_write\_seq\_list'***. This tells you
    that the linker failed to find the Fortran standard libraries. For
    the GNU compilers, you need for example the flag `-lgfortran`, while
    the Intel compiler requires you to add ... To add these flags, set
    the `LDFLAGS` before you run `configure`:

    \# GNU export LDFLAGS=-lgfortran

    \# Intel export LDFLAGS=-lifcore

    On the Python API side, I considered adding these flags to the
    template makefile or offering an API function to add them manually
    to the makefile from the Python script. I dropped these ideas, as
    these libraries are specific to the build and hardware system and
    have nothing to do with a particular application.

-   **The complains about some routines/classes from the technical
    architectures, but they are definitely there**. GCC/C++ is extremely
    sensitive when it comes to the order of the libraries used. You
    might have to edit your makefile manually to get a "valid" ordering
    that works. My recommendation is that you always links from the most
    abstract library to the lowest level lib. In one  project, e.g., the
    following order worked whereas all others resulted in linker errors:
    `-lExaHyPE2Core2d_asserts`, `-lToolboxLoadBalancing2d_asserts`,
    `-lPeano4Core2d_asserts`, `-lTarch_asserts`.

-   To be continued ...

Debugging
---------

-   **I can't see my log/trace statements.** There's a whole set of
    things that can go wrong.

    -   Check that your log filter specifies the **class name**. In
        Peano 3, there was the opportunity to filter messages out on a
        routine level. For efficiency reasons, I had to remove this. So
        now you can only filter per class. Ensure you don't specify
        methods. If you do so, Peano 4 thinks your method name is a
        (sub)class name and won't apply your filter.

    -   Ensure that your main code is translated with a reasonable debug
        flag. The makefile has to translate your code with
        `-DPeanoDebug=4` (or 1 if you are only interested in tracing).

    -   Ensure you link to libraries which are built with a reasonable
        debug level. For this, run your code and search for the header

        build: 2d, no mpi, C++ threading, debug level=4

        on the terminal. Again, has to be 1 or 4 at least.

    -   Ensure you have a whitelist entry for the routine you are
        interested in. Ensure that it is the right level (`TargetDebug`
        or `TargetTrace`, respectively):

        tarch::logging::LogFilter::getInstance().addFilterListEntry(
        tarch::logging::LogFilter::FilterListEntry(
        tarch::logging::LogFilter::FilterListEntry::TargetInfo,
        tarch::logging::LogFilter::FilterListEntry::AnyRank,
        \"examples::algebraicmg\",
        tarch::logging::LogFilter::FilterListEntry::WhiteListEntry ) );

    -   If you are still unsure which log filters are active, insert

        tarch::logging::LogFilter::getInstance().printFilterListToCout();

        into your code and see which entries it does enlist.

-   **gdb says "not in executable format: file format not recognized"**.
    automake puts the actual executable into a `.libs` directory and
    creates bash scripts invoking those guys. Change into `.libs` and
    run gdb directly on the executable. Before you do so, ensure that
    `LD_LIBRARY_Path` points to the directory containing the libraries.
    Again, those guys are stored in a `.libs` subdirectory, so the
    library path should point to that subdirectory.

-   **The interesting details are hidden in the debugger. It says
    something about inlined and doesn't give proper line numbers**. I
    recommend that you set `CXXFLAGS` to `-fno-inline` (Intel), rerun
    the `configure` script and recompile. This should stop the compiler
    from inlining too aggressive.

-   To be continued ...

MPI troubleshooting
-------------------

-   **My compile fails with a message like the one below**:

    This is a know error with MPI installations that do not support
    one-sided MPI.  uses one-sided MPI to raise/report (non-critical)
    exceptions accross MPI boundaries. Non-critical means that the code
    continues to finish the current compute step, dumps the solution
    (for debugging) and then terminates. It does not terminate
    immediately. If you disable one-sided MPI (pass
    `CXXFLAGS=-DnoMPISupportsSingleSidedCommunication` to your
    `configure` call), then such a non-critical assertion will also
    terminate immediately.

-   **My code crashes unexpectedly with malloc/free errors**. Please
    rerun your code with Intel MPI and the flag `-check_mpi`. You should
    not get any error reports. If you do, we have had serious problems
    as some of Peano's classes use `MPI_C_BOOL` or `MPI_CXX_BOOL`. They
    seem not be supported properly.

-   **With more and more ranks, I suddenly run into timeouts**.  has a
    built-in timeout detection which you can alter by changing the
    timeout threshold. This is done via the `–timeout XXX` parameter.
    The other thing is that  subdivides the partition such that each
    core (!) gets one SFC-fragment. For smallish problems, this can lead
    to a massive overhead, as the partitions are too small. In this
    case, you either have to restrict the threads per rank via
    `–threads XXX` (this is problematic, as it effectively reduces the
    code's efficiency) or you instruct your load balancing not to split
    up that often. See the documentation of your particular load
    balancing strategy.

-   To be continued ...

OpenMP troubleshooting
----------------------

-   **My linker fails, as OpenMP or pthreads are not found**. We see
    this frequently with GNU compilers. Ensure that your `LDFLAGS` holds
    `-fopenmp` when you run `configure`.

-   **My code uses only one core**. If you pass
    `–with-multithreading=omp` to `configure`, ensure that you add all
    flags that your compiler needs to `CXXFLAGS`, too. If you compile
    requires you to pass `-openmp`, e.g., to make it understand OpenMP
    pragmas, then you have to add `CXXFLAGS=-openmp` to your configure
    invocation. We've seen multiple times with GNU that the GNU compiler
    translates your codd if you use `–with-multithreading=omp`, but
    effectively ignores the OpenMP pragmas as you forgot to specify
    `CXXFLAGS=-openmp`. You end up with core libraries which do not
    support multithreading or even deadlock.

-   **My code hangs**. Check how many threads OpenMP tries to book. You
    find this information at the startup where you typically obtain a
    message similar to

    Peano 4 (C) www.peano-framework.org build: 2d, no mpi, omp (8
    threads), debug level=2

    The number of threads should not be bigger than the physical threads
    and I strongly recommend not to count hyperthreads as real threads.
    If you find that you have to reduce the thread count (as your system
    by default uses SMT), pass in `–threads X` to reduce the thread
    count to `X` manually. Your initial notification will continue to
    report all SMT threads, but there will be a message immediately
    after that clarifies that you manually reduce the thread count. If
    you work in a SLURM/batch environment, changing the thread/core
    count in the batch script might be more convenient.

-   **I run into linker errors like
    `undefined reference to ‘omp_get_mapped_ptr’`**. This happens if
    your system requires you to specify an OpenMP target when you
    translate with GPU offloading support. Compare to the examples on
    page .

-   To be continued ...

SYCL troubleshooting
--------------------

-   ** always uses all GPUs, but I don't want to use all the (virtual)
    devices**.  uses, by default, all devices that are visible and
    associates one SYCL queue with each device. Most DPC++ runtimes can
    however filter the number of visible devices. See the Intel
    documentation, for example, at
    <https://intel.github.io/llvm-docs/EnvironmentVariables.html>. A
    simple

    does the trick.

-   To be continued ...

External tools
--------------

-   **The Intel tools yield invalid or messed up results**. Ensure you
    have built your code with
    `-DTBB_USE_ASSERT -DTBB_USE_THREADING_TOOLS`. Please consult the
    supercomputer/tool remarks on page .

-   **I struggle to configure  with Otter**. First, ensure you add
    `–with-otter` to your configure call. If the configure fails, run
    through the following checks one by one:

    -   Ensure that you have added
        `CXXFLAGS="... -Iyour-path/otter/include"` to your `CXXFLAGS`.

    -   Ensure that you have added `LIBS="... -lotter-serial -lotf2"` to
        your libraries. Depending on your system, you might need the
        explicit linking to `otf2` or not.

    -   Ensure that you have added
        `LDFLAGS="... –Lyour-path/otter/build/lib -L/opt/otf2/lib"` Once
        again, you might need the explicit OTF2 link or not.

    -   Add the libraries to your library path
        (`LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$LD_LIBRARY_PATH:your-path/otter/build/lib:/opt/otf2/lib`)
        as the checks otherwise will fail.

-   To be continued ...

Errors with particular software stacks
--------------------------------------

-   **My unit tests fail in the NodeTest**. We have found that GCC's STL
    is buggy (confirmed in version 9.3.0), although we do not know if
    this bug is found within the `unordered_set`, the `bitset` or the
    `pair` implementation. Anyway, a newer version of GCC (12 for
    example) seem to fix this issue. If you use Intel's toolchain or
    literally any LLVM, you might have to manually load a newer GNU
    version after you've loaded your core compiler.

